  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 127 PM MDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nThe Wednesday through Friday timeframe continues to come into better \nfocus regarding an approaching Pacific storm system and its \nincreased risk of thunderstorms and flash flood potential. The upper \nlevel jet pattern over the region becomes sharply amplified \nWednesday as a 557-559dm H5 closed low digs down the CA coastline \nwith a ridge of high pressure holding its ground over the TX Big \nBend area and northward over eastern NM and the TX/OK Panhandles. \nThe first plume of subtropical moisture being advected northward \nfrom the EPAC and Gulf of California helps yield the first round of \nrobust showers and storms over northern AZ and into northwestern NM \nand the Four Corners and Navajo Nation area. While ample shear and \nmoisture with modest instability of ~400-600 J/kg of SBCAPE will \nallow for a marginal risk of fast moving strong to severe storms \nover western NM, there is also a marginal to slight risk for storms \npassing over the same areas repeatedly producing locally heavy \nrainfall threatening flash flooding. Overall thunderstorm activity \nlooks to shift steadily back westward into eastern AZ Wednesday \nnight as SW-NE flow b/w the H5 low and H5 ridge becomes more \nmeridional.\n\nThursday sees the peak potential for excessive rainfall threatening \nflash flooding over the western half to two-thirds of the state. A \nsteady stream of subtropical moisture from a combination of a modest \nAR over the EPAC and a chunk of post-Trudy moisture gets pulled over \nAZ and western NM, spiking PWAT's to the 0.90\" to 1.10\" range. These \nwould be daily record PWAT values for mid-to-late October. As the H5 \nlow comes ashore thru the day Thursday, there remains uncertainty \nregarding the timing of a vortmax feature rounding its southern \nperiphery and over the Desert Southwest. Some uncertainty also \nremains with how much cloud cover there may be, limiting daytime \nheating and instability thru the day Thursday. Much of the \nsubtropical moisture associated from the aforementioned AR and \nremnants of Trudy will be in the mid-to-upper levels, potentially \nresulting in abundant cloud cover. Nevertheless, the synoptic setup \nwith large scale ascent and divergent flow aloft will allow for a \nstream of fast moving thunderstorms to flow SW-NE over western NM \nThursday afternoon, shifting into the Rio Grande Valley favoring \nThursday evening and night, and along the central mountain chain and \neastern plains by Friday morning. Depending on storm training and \nhow instability and moisture levels play out, much of the \nnorthwestern half of NM will favor 0.50\" to 1.00\" for event total \nrainfall with higher amounts of 0.75\" to 2.00\" over the higher \nterrain of western and northern NM where orographics will enhance \nlift. Locally higher amounts of 2+\" will favor those higher terrain \nareas of the Gila NF and Tusas Mts northward into the CO San Juans. \n\nFriday favors storms shifting over the eastern plains of NM. \nSoutheasterly flow in the boundary layer combined with a shearing \nwind profile and abundant moisture will allow for a threat of severe \nstorms in this area thru the day Friday. Thereafter, considerable \nuncertainty exists regarding the track and evolution of the upper \nlevel troughing pattern. Whether it's an AI deterministic run, \nnumerical model run, or an ensemble suite of guidance, solutions \nrange from a progressive exit of the upper low out over the Great \nPlains to a segment lagging behind over NM/AZ spinning up further \nmoisture that continues precipitation chances at least for central \nand eastern NM into the weekend.",
